[Applause]  [Applause]  thank you very much  first of all  madam president chaputova  prime minister hegal dear edward  prime minister  karen  kristianis what a pleasure to see you  here and i see the prime minister of  georgia too  ladies and gentlemen excellencies  before i start to reflect  on what we heard from president  zielenski  i want to thank  this  country slovakia  for your  generosity and solidarity with the  ukrainian refugees you're receiving here  it's almost 700 000  you've opened your hearts you've opened  your minds  history will never forget  your gratitude your solidarity and the  shining example you are so many many  thanks for that  we have just heard president zielenski  and it has been now  15 weeks  that  russia has invaded ukraine an innocent  neighbor  and we have learned so much  about the tenacity  of the ukrainian soul  the people of ukraine have found courage  and adversity we've just listened again  and witnessed again that and strength in  each other  and even as this brutal bumping and  shelling is going on  they've never left hope  they  have never lost sight  of what it is all about  it is about shaping the future of  ukraine  and the question indeed how would will  ukraine look like in 5 or 10 or 15 years  and the  impressive element in it is that the  ukrainian people know exactly  what they want their country to be  they want freedom  they want sovereignty  they want security  they want to fulfill the incredible  potential of this beautiful country  they want to shape their future the  future on their own  and they want to be part of our european  family  and they will at no  cost let kremlin stand in their way  i think  this ukrainian resolve  is really inspiring for the whole world  i was like you madam president  in kiev  right before easter in bucha  i've  met  survivors who were barely able to find  words for what they have suffered  i've seen the mass graves  i've seen the body bags  lined up next to a church  i have seen this  trail of blood and destruction  caused by the russian army  and yet  the ukrainians people desire to resume  their lives after that terror is  stronger than anything else  in just one month  they built a new railway bridge  reconnecting  the two towns  to kiev  and the situation is still dire without  any question  but they are taking back their towns  they are taking back their lives  disrespect of what is happening on the  ground and this is so impressive  ukrainians want to return to the path  that leads towards the future they dream  of  towards a prosperous ukraine  a peaceful country  and i want all of them to know  that our european union stands by their  side  we will do everything possible in our  power  so that ukrainians can once again be the  masters of their own future  first  we have to support them to overcome this  war  second they badly need financial relief  and third  we have to accompany ukrainians in  pursuing their path towards the european  union and these are the very practical  three topics i want to speak about  ladies and gentlemen  ukrainians must win this war  and putin  must  witness  that for him it is a severe strategic  failure  it must be clear  that invading a sovereign country comes  with a massive price tag for the  aggressor  we are all standing up for ukraine  and therefore we are not only providing  military aid to a country under attack  i see here the leaders in the room and i  know of your work here in this field in  bilateral support of military equipment  but also the european level for the  first time ever in our history  is providing financial support for  buying military equipment for the  ukrainians  we are mobilizing our full economic  power  putin has mobilized his armed forces  we are mobilizing our economic power  with the powerful sanctions  that we have imposed  we have imposed economic sanctions on  any kind of goods that might  be necessary for modernizing or  diversifying the russian economy and  these sanctions are biting they're  grinding their teeth into the russian  economy  we're paralyzing the russian central  bank's capacity  to raise capital  we're cutting off russian banks from the  swift system  we are getting rid and we have to get  rid of our dependency of russian fossil  fuels  this is  the deepest cut  we can do  to the russian economy because the  russian economy is not a very modern  economy it's not a very diversified  economy but it is a economy based on  fossil fuels so if we want to hit  the kremlin's war chest  if we want  to drain  poutine's war chest then we have to get  rid of our dependency on russian fossil  fuels we have gotten rid of coal already  we are working hard now to ban up to 90  percent of  russian oil  and we have to get rid of russian gas  and for that  distinguished  ladies and gentlemen  we have put out our plan our roadmap  this is repower eu  it's a plan of 300 billion euros  that is composed of on one hand energy  saving that sounds so simple but it's a  powerful tool  if we would only lower  in  the heating for example two degrees  celsius or if there is an air  conditioning  increase it by two degrees celsius  in the european union  this is the whole supply  we're getting through north stream one  at the moment being russian gas only two  degrees  everybody can contribute everybody can  give his or her share  to reduce the dependency on russian gas  second we have to diversify away  from russia and russian gas to our  trustworthy and reliable suppliers like  for example the united states but also  others so we've now formed a joint  platform to purchase together  gas elsewhere in the world because we  know we also have as 27 a huge market  power  and the third topic  is the most important one  that is we have to massively invest  in renewable energy  renewable energy we all know it's good  to fight climate change  but it is also good  to fight  the kremlin's aggression against ukraine  because renewable energy  will replace  russian gas  every single kilowatt hour we get from  solar wind biomass you name it hydrogen  is  a kilowatt hour that we will be reducing  our dependency  on russian fossil fuels  and therefore we know that renewable  energy as i said is not only good for  our climate it is also good for our  independence  and it is also good for our security  energy our supply  and they are homegrown  and they create  good and quality jobs here in the  european union  and then we have to do everything  possible  to support  ukraine  to keep up its economy  according to the united nations  ukraine's economy output might fall by  50 percent this year  yet millions of ukraine still  need to make their living  so to support them  we have suspended all duties and all  quotas on ukrainian goods  and we are setting up solidarity lanes  now  to try to get out of the country as much  wheat or cereals or corn and  agricultural products as we can  because you know that putin is blocking  the export of wheat  via the black sea  it is atrocious to see that that  deliberately deliberately putin is  blocking the wheat supply of the world  50 of the world food program  comes from ukraine's wheat we  desperately need that weed but he's  making  his he's driving his war  um  in taking not the slightest  responsibility for being the one who one  day has to stand up for the fact that  people are dying of hunger and famine  will be big again and hunger will be a  driving motor again in the world  he is deliberately bombarding warehouses  with ukrainian wheat at the moment being  we have 20 million tons of ukrainian  wheat stuck in ukraine so we have to do  everything to get it out via our green  lanes  this is not trivial  but it is absolutely worth every single  effort  we know that ukraine could literally  feed the world  about two-thirds of the country are  covered with the so-called black earth  one of the most fertile souls soils of  the world  and  there's this beautiful ukrainian flag  which symbolizes the yellow fields  under blue blue sky  this is ukraine and we want this ukraine  to have a future  and this leads me  to my third point  that is economic support  and reconstruction  ukraine needs economic  and financial support now  we know that at the moment being ukraine  needs per month 5 billion euros  just to make it  to pay the pensions  to pay the salaries  to have basic services  continuing like health service for  example  so we need to set up a mechanism  that there is the basic support  financial support of around about five  billion euros per month for ukraine  g7 started  and put out nine billion already seven  and a half billion of those are from our  american friends  and one billion from germany in grants  we the european level  proposed now to match this  and to put up an extraordinary macro  financial assistance we're discussing  that in the european council but this is  the minimum we can do to support ukraine  to make it through the next month  financially  but what is  also as important as the now  is that we start to think and president  zielinski did it on screen  that we start to think  now about reconstructing this beautiful  country ukraine  and for that  we want to build a platform  and president celinski spoke partially  about it we want to create a platform  where all these initiatives that are out  there  be it for example from our american  friends but also world bank imf oecd g20  you name it bilateral countries  all these initiatives and  financial support  are channeled on a platform  where we together  decide on the direction to travel  this platform is led and owned by  ukraine because it's ukraine who decides  on its future  but together with them  we want to engage  in from the very beginning on  to shape this platform in a way  that we have  investment  with reform  the european commission  will stand by ukrainian side with all  the track record we have with all the  experience we have  to help them that the investment is  effective combined with the necessary  reforms that then will of course pave  the way into the european union  it is for us important that there are  reforms on the  administrative capacity for example  on a strong rule of law  on an independent judiciary  always the fight against corruption  but also creating a conducive  environment  for the business sector so that  investors  have confidence and trust and come back  and invest in ukraine  ukrainians are fighting so bravely  because they know exactly what kind of  future they want  a future  of a free and open society  belonging to a strong democratic  community  they want to join the european union  we've heard it over and over again i  think  it's not only our strategic interests  but it is our moral duty to make it  possible that they join our european  union and that we pave the path towards  our union  [Applause]  and let me tell you why i think it is so  important to combine investment and  reform  because of course our standards and  conditions  in an eu accession process  must be met  there is neither a wild card nor  shortcut  but how fast  how quickly  how the speed of that such a process is  this is depending on the country itself  and our support  and i'll never forget  prime minister dear edward that you told  me  what a change it made in slovakia  when the country at a certain point in  time  decided to be serious about the  accession of process towards the  european union and then you told me that  the european commission sent expertise  advice a team that worked with you day  and night to do the reforms that were  necessary of course there was a certain  amount of investment  and you made it within a few years  and this is important  once again no shortcuts no wildcard  standards and conditions have to be met  because this is true for all of us in  the european union and we know on a  daily basis how hard it is to keep these  standards up among us 27.  so that has to be  but it's up to us how much we support  how much we invest and it's in the hands  of our ukrainian friends  what they are making out of it  and ladies and gentlemen  after all  i always think when i listen to  president zielenski and all our  ukrainian friends  and the last days i met many young  people from ukraine young delegates from  ukraine  ukraine's longing  and its aspirations  are basically  a reflection of what we have achieved  together  over the past 77  years and if i may pick up the words  that president zelensky spoke i think it  was the last question on the  responsibility he has he said this is  not a burden  this is a great responsibility  and this should be our motto  supporting ukraine on its path towards  the european union it is not a burden  it is our historic responsibility long  live europe's lava ukrainian  [Laughter]  [Applause]

